# Internet Visualizer -- environment variables
#
# There are no secrets here and there is nothing to fill in to run the product. Every
# module is a deterministic client-side simulation, and even the one module that can
# reach a real network -- Network Diagnostics' Live mode -- needs no credential: its
# three lookups go to public, unauthenticated endpoints that are named in the code
# rather than configured here. See "Deliberately not configurable" at the bottom, which
# is the more important half of this file.
#
# Copy to .env.local for local development. Never commit .env.local.
# On Vercel, set these in Project Settings -> Environment Variables instead.


# --- Site identity -----------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The canonical origin, used by src/app/sitemap.ts, src/app/robots.ts and the
# `metadataBase` every canonical link, `og:url` and Open Graph image URL is resolved
# against.
#
# Optional. Unset, the app falls back in this order (src/lib/site.ts):
#
#   1. VERCEL_PROJECT_PRODUCTION_URL -- the project's production hostname, which Vercel
#      sets on every deployment including previews. This is why a preview's canonical
#      links point at production rather than at a hostname that will stop existing.
#   2. VERCEL_URL -- this specific deployment, used only before a production domain
#      exists.
#   3. http://localhost:3000
#
# Set it as soon as the project has a custom domain: nothing above knows about one, and
# Vercel's variables keep reporting the *.vercel.app hostname while the custom domain
# serves the same deployment. A canonical pointing at the wrong one of two hostnames
# splits the site's search identity in half.
#
# A scheme is optional (https:// is assumed) and a trailing slash is stripped.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL="https://internet-visualizer.vercel.app"


# --- Content Security Policy -------------------------------------------------------
#
# The policy itself lives in src/lib/securityHeaders.ts, with the argument for each
# directive; these two variables only decide how strictly it is applied. Both are read
# at build time by next.config.ts, so changing either needs a redeploy, not a restart.
#
# CSP_MODE
#   "enforce" (default, and the default for anything unrecognised) sends
#   Content-Security-Policy: a violation is blocked.
#   "report-only" sends Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only instead: a violation is
#   reported and then allowed. Nothing else about the policy changes.
#
#   The rollout the phase doc asks for, in the order to do it:
#
#     1. Deploy a preview with CSP_MODE=report-only.
#     2. Run `npx playwright test e2e/security.spec.ts` against it, or open the pages
#        by hand with the console visible. The spec listens for the
#        `securitypolicyviolation` DOM event, which fires in both modes -- so it proves
#        the policy is clean *before* the policy is allowed to break anything.
#     3. Remove the variable (or set it to "enforce") and deploy to production.
#
#   Doing this again is the correct response to any change to the policy, to a new
#   third-party dependency, or to anything that starts loading a font, image, script or
#   stylesheet from somewhere new.
#
# CSP_MODE="report-only"
#
# CSP_REPORT_URI
#   Optional. When set, `report-uri <url>` is appended to the policy and browsers POST
#   a JSON report of each violation there as well as logging it to the console. Useful
#   during a report-only window on a deployment nobody is watching by hand; there is no
#   receiving endpoint in this repository, so this expects an external collector.
#
# CSP_REPORT_URI="https://example.report-collector.test/csp"


# --- Provided by Vercel; do not set these yourself ----------------------------------
#
# Listed because src/lib/site.ts reads them and a reader should not have to grep for
# where an unset variable's value comes from.
#
#   VERCEL_ENV                      "production" | "preview" | "development".
#                                   Anything other than "production" makes robots.txt
#                                   disallow everything and the layout emit a noindex,
#                                   so previews cannot be indexed as duplicates of the
#                                   real site. Absent -- a local build, or a
#                                   self-hosted deployment -- counts as production.
#   VERCEL_PROJECT_PRODUCTION_URL   The project's production hostname, no scheme.
#   VERCEL_URL                      This deployment's hostname, no scheme.


# --- Deliberately not configurable --------------------------------------------------
#
# An earlier draft of this file offered DOH_RESOLVER_URL, RDAP_BOOTSTRAP_URL,
# DIAGNOSTICS_RATE_LIMIT_PER_MINUTE and DIAGNOSTICS_TIMEOUT_MS. No code ever read them,
# and they are gone rather than wired up, because each would have taken something away:
#
#   * The DNS-over-HTTPS resolver and the RDAP bootstrap origin are constants in
#     src/core/net/diagnostics.ts, and the outbound guard checks each call's origin
#     against them. An allow-list of one is a claim the UI can make honestly and a
#     reader can verify in a second; an allow-list read from the environment is a claim
#     about whatever someone last typed into a dashboard. LiveDisclosure shows the exact
#     URL a handler will request *before* it requests it, and that is only honest
#     because the panel and the handler call the same function over the same constant.
#
#   * The rate limits (src/core/net/ratelimit.ts) and the outbound timeout and body cap
#     (src/core/net/guard.ts) are ceilings, not settings. A caller may narrow them; an
#     environment variable could widen them, which is the one direction that turns a
#     bounded lookup into an unbounded one.
#
# If the rate limiter ever needs to hold across serverless instances rather than per
# instance, the seam is documented at the top of ratelimit.ts -- that is an Upstash
# Redis connection string, and it would be added here when it exists.
